  "semantic_description": {
    "level_1_elemental": "Scatter of 24 customer accounts: x = acquisition cost in dollars (roughly $980\u2013$4,780), y = first-year lifetime value in dollars (roughly $4,300\u2013$9,800), each point colored by one of four sales regions (West, Midwest, South, Northeast).",
    "level_2_statistical": "The LTV/CAC relationship is weak-to-negative: the cheapest-to-acquire West and Midwest accounts return the highest LTV (LTV/CAC roughly 4\u20135x), while the costliest Northeast accounts return the lowest LTV (near or below break-even at ~1.3x). A least-squares fit of LTV on CAC would slope gently downward, pulled by the Northeast cluster.",
    "level_3_perceptual": "Four color bands separate cleanly left-to-right along the x-axis: West (cheap, high-value) sits upper-left, Midwest just right of it, South in the middle, and the red Northeast cluster isolated in the lower-right \u2014 high cost, low value. The eye reads the Northeast as an outlier band that drags the relationship negative.",
    "level_4_contextual": "If acquisition spend tracked returns, costlier regions should yield higher LTV; the Northeast inverts this, signaling that incremental spend there is buying unprofitable accounts and should be reallocated toward the West/Midwest motion.",
    "short_alt": "Scatter of acquisition cost vs first-year value by region; the costly Northeast cluster returns the lowest value.",
